@h1 Tutorial: build a report, end to end h1@

The other Get Started pages teach one idea at a time. This one proves they
compose. We'll start with an empty file and finish with a printed PDF — a short
illustrated report called @em The Lamp Keeper em@ — adding exactly one
construct per stage. Every stage builds and shows its result, so you're never
looking at something that only works three steps from now.

Have @code wit code@ installed (see @a |href /docs/start/install/| Install a@) and
a terminal open. Let's go.

@h2 Stage 1 — Prose h2@

Make a file called @code report.wit code@ and write three plain paragraphs. No
markup — a blank line starts each new paragraph.

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken aloud in eleven days.

He had not noticed, until his own voice surprised him.

The lamp burned steady through the storm — the kind of weather
that, in another life, would have called for company.
pre@@@

Build it to a web page:

@@@pre
wit build report.wit -o report.html
pre@@@

Open @code report.html code@. Three clean paragraphs in the built-in theme — no
CSS, no boilerplate, and it already looks like a document. That's your baseline.
Everything from here just adds structure on top.

@h2 Stage 2 — A heading h2@

Give the report a title. Add one line at the very top:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@
pre@@@

Rebuild with the same command and refresh. The line became a real
@@@code <h1> code@@@ heading, set in the theme's display size. The mechanic here is
a @strong node strong@: an opener @@@code @h1 code@@@, the text, and a matching
closer @@@code h1@ code@@@.

@aside(class note) 💡 Miss the closer — write @@@code @h1 The Lamp Keeper code@@@
with no @@@code h1@ code@@@ — and the build stops with
@code report.wit:1:1: E_UNCLOSED_NODE: unclosed @h1 code@, pointing at the opener.
A node is a @em pair em@. If a build ever fails on an unclosed node, look for
the closer you dropped. aside@

@h2 Stage 3 — Emphasis h2@

Mark a couple of words. In the first paragraph, make @code eleven days code@ bold
and @code voice code@ italic:

@@@pre
The keeper had not spoken aloud in *eleven days*. He had not
noticed, until his own _voice_ surprised him.
pre@@@

Rebuild. @code *eleven days* code@ is now @strong bold strong@ and
@code _voice_ code@ is @em italic em@. Two marks, on purpose — restraint keeps
prose readable.

The marks wrap a @strong single token strong@, which is why they never fire by
accident. Everyday arithmetic and identifiers stay exactly as typed — no escaping
needed:

@@@pre
He multiplied 5*6*7 in his head and the file_name_here stayed plain.
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 To set a word both bold @em and em@ italic, put bold on the
outside: @@@code *_both_* code@@@ works and gives you
@@@code <strong><em>both</em></strong> code@@@. The reverse, @@@code _*both*_ code@@@,
leaves the inner @code * code@ as a literal asterisk — a real asymmetry worth
remembering. Reach for @@@code *_…_* code@@@ when you want both. aside@

@h2 Stage 4 — A list h2@

The keeper tended several stations. Add a subheading and a bulleted list beneath
the paragraphs:

@@@pre
@h2 The stations he tended h2@

@ul
  @li Dunmore Head li@
  @li Carrick Point li@
  @li Ines Rock li@
ul@
pre@@@

Rebuild. You get an @@@code <ul> code@@@ of three @@@code <li> code@@@ items. The
indentation is only for your eye — scope comes from the @@@code @ul … ul@ code@@@
and @@@code @li … li@ code@@@ pairs. For a numbered list, swap @@@code @ul code@@@
for @@@code @ol code@@@ and the very same items render @@@code <ol> code@@@ with
numbers.

@aside(class note) 💡 If you ever open a list with a @code ~ code@ comment as its
first line and no blank line after it, the following items collapse into a single
stray paragraph — a known parser quirk. The one-keystroke fix is a blank line
after any comment inside a node. aside@

@h2 Stage 5 — A table h2@

Now something Markdown can't do without dropping into HTML: a captioned table,
written inline. Add this below the list:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Site, Status], [Dunmore Head, operational], [Carrick Point, maintenance]]| |caption Station status|
pre@@@

Rebuild. Wit builds a full @@@code <table> code@@@ — a @@@code <caption> code@@@, a
@@@code <thead> code@@@ from the first row, and a @@@code <tbody> code@@@ from the
rest. The rows ride in a parameter because they're structured metadata; the table
itself is the node.

@aside(class note) 💡 The cells in @@@code |rows [[…]]| code@@@ are separated by
commas, and there is no way to escape a comma inside them. A caption or cell that
contains a comma — @code Station status, final code@ — will split into extra
cells and misalign the table. Keep cell text comma-free (use @em and em@, a
semicolon, or a dash), or move the data into a
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| record collection a@ and generate the table
from it. aside@

@h2 Stage 6 — A figure h2@

Give the report an image with a real caption. Add a figure at the end (any
@code .png code@ next to the file will do for @code src code@):

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
  @figcaption The lamp that never went dark. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

Rebuild. You get a semantic @@@code <figure> code@@@ wrapping the
@@@code <img> code@@@ and a @@@code <figcaption> code@@@ — accessible, and still
readable in the source. The image's @code src code@ and @code alt code@ are
metadata in parameters; the caption is content in a body.

@aside(class note) 💡 One cosmetic quirk: the block @@@code @figcaption code@@@
currently renders wrapped in an extra @@@code <p> code@@@ inside the figure. It's
harmless and the caption displays correctly — don't be surprised by the tag in
the output. aside@

Your @code report.wit code@ now reads, in full:

@@@pre
@h1 The Lamp Keeper h1@

The keeper had not spoken aloud in *eleven days*. He had not
noticed, until his own _voice_ surprised him.

The lamp burned steady through the storm — the kind of weather
that, in another life, would have called for company.

@h2 The stations he tended h2@

@ul
  @li Dunmore Head li@
  @li Carrick Point li@
  @li Ines Rock li@
ul@

@table |rows [[Site, Status], [Dunmore Head, operational], [Carrick Point, maintenance]]| |caption Station status|

@figure
  @img |src ./lamp.png| |alt The keeper's lamp|
  @figcaption The lamp that never went dark. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

One plain text file — prose, a heading, emphasis, a list, a table, and a figure —
and it has built to HTML at every single step along the way.

@h2 Stage 7 — Save it in git h2@

The source @em is em@ the artifact: a plain, diffable text file. Put it under
version control:

@@@pre
git init
git add report.wit
git commit -m "The Lamp Keeper — first draft"
pre@@@

Now every draft you send a reader is versioned, and every change is a readable
diff — because the source is text, not a binary document. This is the whole
writer's workflow: edit the file, rebuild, commit.

@aside(class note) 💡 There is no @code --watch code@ flag to rebuild on save. The
build is fast enough to re-run by hand, and if you want it automatic you can wrap
it in a shell loop:
@code while true; do wit build report.wit -o report.html; sleep 1; done code@.
An auto-watcher is on the wish list, not in the tool today. aside@

@h2 Stage 8 — Build to PDF h2@

Finally, the reader-ready copy. Change the output extension:

@@@pre
wit build report.wit -o report.pdf
pre@@@

Wit renders the same styled HTML and paginates it into a PDF through a headless
copy of Chrome. Open @code report.pdf code@ — a clean, printable document with
your heading, your list, your table, and your captioned figure, laid out across
pages.

Because PDF leans on Chrome, it needs a system Chrome or Chromium installed. If
none is found, the build says so plainly:

@@@pre
wit build: no headless Chrome/Chromium found for PDF output.
Install Google Chrome, or point WIT_CHROME at a browser executable.
pre@@@

The fix is in the message: install Chrome, or set @code WIT_CHROME code@ to a
browser you already have. That's it — you took an empty file to a printed report,
adding one construct at a time, and the source is a single versioned text file
that also produces HTML and Markdown whenever you ask.

@h2 See also h2@

Each stage here operationalises a Get Started page: prose and building in
@a |href /docs/start/first-document/| Your first document a@, the node mechanic in
@a |href /docs/start/nodes/| Nodes and core vocabulary a@, and the layers in
@a |href /docs/start/mental-model/| the mental model a@. If a stage errored,
@a |href /docs/start/troubleshooting/| Troubleshooting a@ has the fix. Coming from
another tool? See @a |href /docs/start/migration-markdown/| Migration from Markdown a@
or @a |href /docs/start/migration-latex/| Migration from LaTeX a@.
